so I thought that this class would allow  me an opportunity to first off create  and some sort of application or products  that would be interesting and enjoyable  to make and more importantly have actual  impact it would be working with someone  and doing something that would directly  benefit them and a lot of them to  improve their daily lives in some way  and I think that gives it a lot more  meaning a lot more power and makes it a  lot more enjoyable of an experience to  create  um so I really wasn't entirely sure what  it all would entail initially I thought  that I knew we'd be working with a  client I knew we'd be creating a product  but I didn't realize all of the focus  that would be put on the looking at the  client the contextual analysis the  figuring out what it is that the client  can do wants to do is able to do and  there's a lot of focus on features other  than directly creating the piece of  assistive technology and I didn't expect  that that would be the case but I  realized it was critical that that was  there because they really make something  successful you have to understand all of  the context surrounding it and to do  something that is really useful you have  to know what it is that the client needs  that's applicable both an assistive  technology and just in an working toward  for any client so I came into this class  hoping that I would be able to work on a  project that actually had an impact  because a lot of most of the courses i  had taken at MIT they were a theoretical  and you just work on problem sets and  you hope to apply the things that you  learn after college and so the last  semester and the semester I've been  trying to find courses where I can  actually use those skills like  immediately and like I feel like  practicing those skills is actually  what's going to help me learn them  better and so learning through  experience and that's exactly what I got  out of the stuff i was able to apply the  web design skills that I learned and  also just like the design the whole  design process that we were told about  actually applying into this analogy  well I learned that there was always a  lot more than meets the eye I realized  that there's a lot of I was surprised  working with both our client with  hearing about the other clients in the  class how much they were able to do even  with given their disabilities they were  very well able to do quite a lot of  things that I wouldn't have expected  them to be able to and I realize that  you can't you have to really focus on  what their abilities are in what their  needs are and I think having a client  there in the first place gave us is  level of focus that is normally not  found in creating in our case a software  application we're looking at a  particular user and focusing on what one  particular person wants and that made it  a lot easier to know what direction to  take it rather than having some nebulous  group of our supposed users out there  that we didn't actually know her  interact with  put in a lot of time at the beginning to  understanding the context and the client  and the situation and it's really  tempting to jump right in and start  coding and we didn't do that and I'm  glad we didn't do that because we were  able to understand what it was we were  supposed to be doing and what direction  we needed to take so I think that's the  biggest piece of advice there is to not  start coding right away but make sure  you really understand what you're doing  so we went through a bunch of iterations  first we started off with original we  just discussed with our client what he  wanted what his needs were and then each  of us so we had four people in our group  and we've separated and came up with our  own ideas of what the client might want  and so we came up with paper prototypes  and then we brought these four versions  into the next meeting and discussed work  like our client told us what he liked  about them what did we didn't like about  them and so from that we collaborated  and put those four together into a final  paper prototype and then just kept  iterating from there going back to our  client asking what he liked about it  what he didn't like about it and then  once we had a pretty finalized version  of what we were hoping to get out of  this project we went into actually  starting to code and separating the task  everything  the most challenging aspect was probably  while going through all these iterations  and coding um I kind of want to say it  was the actual implementation so coming  up with the ideas and everything it was  great it was like a lot of creativity  and trying to fix these problems but  then actually going into the coding  portion was when we came into the bugs  of like like how do we actually  implement what we want out of our  project we know like that we want this  certain type of screen there we know how  we want the user to interact with this  but how do we actually do this  technically and that was one of the  challenging parts of the class  I think the blog's were really a time  for reflection and thinking about what  it was we were doing I use the blogs at  least is sort of a way to catalyze my  analysis of the subjects and the  material presented in class and the  things we've been working on so I took  it and really look to analyze some  thought I'd have been having trying to  figure out how to think about one on how  to make computers more accessible and  ways in which that is sort of  fundamental in a lot of the architecture  and how it makes it difficult to do that  we're just analyzing different ways in  which we're able to allow people with  disabilities to be more integrated into  society and how society has to  potentially change their viewpoints a  little bit to allow that happen to a  greater degree  the most engaging asset was definitely  working with all of the people in the  course so my like three other team  members and our client which like I said  if you don't entirely get in other  courses and this one's you're sitting  down with the person who is going to be  using the product and that is really  that was the most engaging because I  knew it was gonna be fruitful and I knew  something was going to come out at the  end  I mean as far as like an education  perspective at least on this i found  this is of course where I was motivated  to do the best I could not by getting a  good grade or even as much about  learning something as it was about I  really wanted to help the client and I  think it's a client-centered focus of  the course that really drives it and  that is the I feel the most important  aspect because when you're working with  someone one-on-one they're relying on  you to create something that you know  it's going to help them you are really  well motivated to do the best you can on  that product and I think that making  sure that that emphasis is retained and  is the most important thing to keep  everyone really engaged in and to get  the best quality of products that is  possible from a class like this and  that's really the best way to make it  happen in my perspective  you're creating a product for your  client but you're also learning more  about a certain person's lifestyle and  so make sure to go beyond what you're  making as a product but get to know your  client as a person and that's definitely  going to be even more fruitful because  then you're not just focused on this one  like product product product but you're  learning more of how does this one  person have to live with their  disabilities and how can you integrate  like so many other things in your  environment to work help them and these  times with people like outside of the  semester
 

